about you  is like your desire to get to the bottom  of a mystery  or um how strong of an advocate  you are for you know people who  who've been harmed or something like  that but then then we're going back to  like communications basics to say like  but when i see you posting things like  this  it makes me angry or it makes me feel  worried because  and then you can fill in what your exact  issue is with sharing that particular  piece of information  um i personally tend to like to give  people  um opportunities to save face especially  if you are in social media forums  this is a friend um you know people that  you've got an audience who are observing  your interactions  i would much rather give people the  benefit of the doubt and a graceful way  out  and then back them into a corner um  and finally the thing that i like to  keep in mind for this was a book that  my co-director of the story collider  introduced me to it's called  radical candor by kim scott and it's the  idea that you care personally  but confront directly and  that for me is how i would actually talk  to  a friend someone who's important in my  life if they were sharing a piece of  propaganda or misinformation  thanks thanks liz um here's another  question  what do you think was the best  communication and the first  communication piece on covid19  um i think that's an impossible question  to answer as opposed but i really  appreciate the  the tenor and intention of it so um  admitting i am biased here because uh  the journalist i'm going to mention is  my husband in fact um uh ed young  at the atlantic has had a whole series  of  long reads about covid and one of my  favorites was  why is this all so confusing um  so what he's doing is attempting to  cover science that is a complex story  that is still unfolding in real time  that we may not have clear answers to  actionable  questions yet um by giving people a  framework  that they can use to understand so it's  not merely  you know he also wrote another piece  called uh everyone thinks they're right  about masks  and this was back when there was a lot  more uncertainty about whether or not we  should be wearing masks  um and instead of trying to say you  should do this or you should do that  he gave um hungry audiences a way to  make sense  of the information that they were seeing  that validates why there is this  difference of opinion  and so i think that kind of work is  outstanding  um the kind of coverage that i think is  damaging is um  uh the kind of like tick-tock journalism  that just covers  new pre-prints uncritically or or worse  you know um report out a press release  from an institution  without any kind of analysis or outside  uh  peer review or the scientific process  because i think that can be exhausting  and it adds to confusion and  misinformation  even if it's well intentioned  all right thanks liz here's another good  question  um sorry can't get my  three now okay so  um someone would like to know  how you establish trust with someone  who has anti-science views that seems to  be the  key to getting them to listen yeah  i mean so let's all let's all be honest  with ourselves  um attempting to get someone to trust  you  in the middle of an argument when  they're a stranger that you've had no  connection with in the past  that's asking a lot especially um  just given that the tenor of arguments  that are happening around  many of these issues um i absolutely  think that the ways  we can and should be doing this is to  become more trustworthy  so i talk about this being honest and  transparent in your answers  admitting where the bounds of your  knowledge are not only to your audience  but also to yourself  as you work to expand them um  identifying the difference between when  you have  a value value-driven or an emotional  response  to a particular part of the conversation  versus what we actually know as  researchers or scientists i think is  important  i i am always a proponent of  building uh relationships with other  people who already are trusted within  their  community who can because we can think  about amplifying other messages  rather than attempting to um one-on-one  build trust with people who have maybe  good reasons not to trust us quite  frankly  i think being able to interrogate why  there is that current lack of trust  and um like i said in the keynote um  recognizing that uh claiming there is a  generalized anti-science  added worldview is not well supported by  any of our available data on how people  actually  are making sense of things most people  want to say that their worldview is  backed up by science um they may  question  the um motivations and behaviors  of people who have conventional  expertise and credibility  but that's legitimate and i think  especially appreciating and recognizing  um those those underlying causes of  whatever distrust might be there  is uh a really important first step and  towards mending a relationship  here's another one uh my experience is  that people react differently to spoken  versus written community  do you have specific advice for  responding and writing rather than in  conversation  oh thank you for making me think about  that  um i don't actually i don't have  research that i can think of  that shows how we would do that um  i tend to feel and this is only based on  my  lived experience that  whether i'm talking to strangers on  social media or to my  friends and family that  the jargon and academic language that i  use to  telegraph expertise and credibility when  i'm  on camera like this or on on stage or in  a professional setting  doesn't always serve me well  and the more that i try to  write and speak the way that i do in  typical conversation  so that um my identity as someone who  cares about science  is not removed from my core identity as  their friend or colleague or daughter  whatever it might be  that it helps integrate these parts of  me uh  it helps position the information that  i'm trying to share  uh with all of who i am  and i do think that that part is  important and i know i'm dodging the  question  here i am partially i'm trying to buy  time and let my brain  tick and just think have i read or seen  anything that would be interesting and  helpful for us  i'm not i'm coming up short but um one  of the things i wanted to mention to  everyone who is watching  is you heard me in the presentation  mention that i have a publix zotero  library  of research publications um  uh and i'm going to look to see if i can  find any in this area  we're going to share that link it'll pop  up in the chat that won't be enduring  but we will also make sure that  we share this with all of you if you are  interested in  the evidence base for this communication  advice  that we are giving here  i think here is another one could you  speak  specifically about how you communicate  regarding issues like  animal experimentation yeah  so  all of these um  i don't know if they polarized because  this isn't a typically a typical example  of a polarized  scientific issue but it is very  emotionally heated  the people who are coming into a  conversation  have a certain set of expectations about  how scientists behave  how they reach their how they do  research how they reach conclusions  and how they're going to behave um  i think one of the powerful things you  can do  is disrupt those expectations  so one thing that we see is people who  have not done  animal research themselves are not um  connected to it personally  they think oh my goodness in order to do  this you must be a  callous cold person who just doesn't  care  about the animals you know in your in  your work  the scientists i know who conduct animal  research that couldn't be farther from  the truth  and typically they attempt to  emotionally distance themselves because  that is what is required  um to do their jobs but also to present  their work to their peers  and but by being able to share  what the toll this takes on them um  and why they still believe it's so  important to do  that helps move into that personalized  storytelling narrative space of  here's what i think about here's what's  going on inside my mind or behind the  scenes and here's why i  choose to take on this sacrifice to make  these decisions  it doesn't mean that everyone's going to  necessarily agree with you  but it certainly shifts the debate away  from  um sort of typical talking points that  people might be much more used to  hearing  um and also discrediting whereas when  you talk about your own choices  and your own experiences um  that has so much more validity because  you're talking about what you yourself  have gone through and believe  thanks um do you have a method for  finding a story to tell  figuring out what will make a good story  oh my goodness  um yes and no  so i am not a natural-born um performer  a lot of my friends and colleagues are  you know they're they're comedians  they're  they're actors they are story  professional storytellers and they are  just incredible wells of information  of stories it seems like they come up  with them all the time  and especially early on in my tenure at  story collider i was so frustrated  because i thought like how can i  how can i do this like i i want to tell  stories i like listening to them  i believe they are important but i've  when i come to myself it's just like  everything i've ever done  escapes my head and i feel stuck  so um there is a  many many time award-winning storyteller  named matthew dix  who has a technique he calls homework  for life  he keeps a spreadsheet i love a good  spreadsheet or a database  and every day he puts down just one  single thing that happens to him that  day that could be the basis  of a story in the future so it's kind of  like journaling  or keeping a diary and he argues that  this helps  give his days more like granularity  especially as he's a parent of young  children he says the days can just blur  the  months and years can go by but by doing  this he remembers those moments  um as they happen things that have  emotional resonance  to him and we often find so the story  collider does in fact offer trainings  and workshops for  faculty researchers scientists in  particular because  we are the people who've never been  trained how to do this and actively  trained against it  and so this is one of the biggest pain  points um in the process and it was  something i really wanted to figure out  how do we do this  part of it is getting out of your own  head and allowing yourself  to be creative free of the judgment that  we typically are you know sort of going  through i know i do this all the time  like  well i could tell a story about that oh  but that's probably not important enough  i or how would i get a message in there  um  put that aside and then we start looking  at strong emotional resonance  where are what are moments in your  scientific career  where you have been enraged  or so curious you couldn't stand it  or shocked or joyful  or sorrowful um we know  emotions are really important in  storytelling for a whole variety of  reasons  but we also experience strong emotions  when the stakes are really high  and this means this makes for good  storytelling that tension when you don't  know how something is going to go  and the consequences are very dire  that is typically a great place to look  for  story content and then honestly the best  thing is  having an outsider that you know like we  use  our team of producers and story coaches  um are trained to do this we're asking  what were you thinking  why did you do that what happened next  um  and being able to see the outside  perspective because we take for granted  so much  that we do and think um and we would  presume  everyone would behave the way that we  behave but that's not true this is what  makes storytelling and science  storytelling  in particular so delightful is that  all of the processing that you're doing  as you face a decision  um the options that you went through and  discarded before you took an action  that shows like the scientific process  in action  that shows your unique and idiosyncratic  approach to the kind of research that  you do and  those are the kinds of things that i  think make for excellent stories whether  they're big  or just tiny little moments in  our careers thanks liz well we are  just about out of time and um just  wanted to ask you one more question  in spite of some scientists will still  think talking to the public is a big  waste of time  how would you answer that i mean  so i really think this is about  an alignment between our behavior and  our expressed values  so on one hand i can make the same  pragmatic arguments that have been made  in so many formats so many times  more powerfully than i can make it today  but if your research is taxpayer funded  you  owe an explanation to the people who are  making this possible  i also think it goes towards the valid  like the sort of social legitimacy  of science as an enterprise if you want  people  to appreciate and support and buy into  this  we need to make them part of it listen  to their concerns  um you know consult them when decisions  need to be  made in a meaningful way and more  important than that too i think there's  another layer of sort of pragmatic  arguments  if as experts you decide  that um it's not worth your time  talking with people you've now seeded  the entire public debate  to those who might very well have  interests very far afield  from yours and and that vacuum is filled  with other voices  i think we have a combination of um  responsibilities some of them are more  on i think a value in moral  grounding others are tactical and  pragmatic  um but i hope that we all recognize we  don't  need to be all engaging  in the same way um  that we have our own particular  approaches and our own particular niches  you know some of you some of us might be  wonderful on tv  does that mean everyone should be on tv  of course not um  but figuring out how you uniquely  can contribute what it is that you are  doing  and why it matters so much i think that  is  um ultimately a social good  well thank you liz this presentation has  been wonderful and  how can people get with you if they're  interested in learning more  yeah so it you can find me on uh twitter  my very unique username is just my name  at liz neely  but more importantly i encourage you to  go to storycollider.org  you can listen to our weekly podcast new  episode drops every friday  you can see our upcoming shows which we  are performing online every friday at  7pm  eastern time um and you can find  more about why and how we undertake this  wonderful mission in the world about  telling true personal stories about  science  to help humanize scientists illustrate  the scientific  process and correct the  you know over representation that we've  had for too long  in science by a very narrow set of  voices  we can do a much better job of  showcasing the reality  of science and research well thanks  again  and i want to remind the audience  there's another communications workshop  tomorrow at 5  30 it's how to use social media to get  out the word on your  brain research thanks again for coming  and thank you liz  thank you all
 

